extends Node

var content = """[font_size=28][b]Vitruvian Man[/b][/font_size]
[i]The Measure of All Things[/i]

[i][color=gray]Summary: Leonardo da Vinci's drawing places the human body at the center of the Circle (divine) and the Square (earthly). It is an assertion of proportion, symmetry, and the ideal. But digital bodies are meshes—collections of vertices that can be distorted, exploded, and reconfigured.[/color][/i]

[hr]

[b]1. The Canonical Proportions[/b]

• The span of the arms is equal to the height.
• The center of the body (navel) is the center of the circle.
• The face is 1/10th of the body height.

These are algorithms of construction. They define the "Normative Body."

[hr]

[b]2. Fourier Body[/b]

In this scene, we see the "Fourier Body." We have decomposed the Vitruvian ideal into frequencies.

What happens if we filter out the high frequencies (details)? We get a smooth, indistinct blob.
What happens if we boost the noise? The body dissolves into a cloud.

[hr]

[b]3. The Glitch in the Ideal[/b]

The Vitruvian Man excludes almost everyone. It excludes the asymmetric, the disabled, the trans, the growing, the dying. It is a static cage of "perfection."

In 3D space, we can break the cage. We can scale the X axis. We can twist the spine. We can map audio to the vertex positions.

[color=green][b]Code[/b][/color]
[color=green][code]
# Breaking the proportion
skeleton.set_bone_scale(arm_bone, Vector3(1.5, 1.0, 1.0))
[/code][/color]

[hr]

[b]4. Queer Morphology[/b]

Queer theory asks: what if the body is not a fixed template, but a fluid site of construction?

By applying wave functions to the body mesh, we move from [b]Being[/b] (static ideal) to [b]Becoming[/b] (fluid process). The body breathes, distorts, and remakes itself in real-time.

[color=orange][b]Critical Note:[/b][/color] The computer allows us to visualize the body not as a sacred temple, but as a flexible topology. We are not stuck in Leonardo's circle. We can shape-shift.

[hr]

[b]End of Sequence.[/b]
"""

